data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 9+, the Cosmos DB provider started throwing by default for synchronous operations, but allowed an opt-in to re-enable sync-over-async behavior. **In EF Core 11 (.NET 11), that opt-in is removed entirely.** The Cosmos DB provider no longer supports synchronous LINQ operations at all.

Every call in your repository that blocks synchronously will throw `InvalidOperationException`:

| Broken Call | Async Replacement |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(...)` | `.FirstAsync(...)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

## Fix: Convert to Async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- There is no workaround — the Cosmos provider's underlying SDK is async-only, and EF Core 11 no longer wraps it.
- You must propagate `async/await` up through your call chain (controllers, services, etc.).
- The relational providers (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, etc.) are unaffected — this is Cosmos-specific.
